# XSH ttyname() and ttyname_r()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# A terminal needs a pseudo-terminal obtained with posix_openpt(), grantpt(),
# unlockpt() and ptsname() (XSI); the name returned is compared with the
# subsidiary device by file identity (stat), not by spelling, because the
# text asks only for a pathname of the terminal. Tests with no terminal are
# Base.
#
# "when BASE|XSI": the requirements are Base; XSI is named only so that the
# driver builds these tests with the XSI feature macro (the pseudo-terminal
# set-up needs it). They run wherever they build, and a set-up that is not
# available is UNRESOLVED.

assertion  XSH.ttyname.001
anchor     functions/ttyname.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ttyname.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       ttyname() on the subsidiary descriptor of a pseudo-terminal
           returns a non-null pointer to a non-empty string which, used as
           a pathname, names the same device as the descriptor (same file,
           or same character device number).
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base.
test       t/001-ttyname-pty.c

assertion  XSH.ttyname.002
anchor     functions/ttyname.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ttyname.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      2001
says       ttyname_r() on the subsidiary descriptor with a large buffer
           returns 0 and stores a non-empty string naming the same device
           as the descriptor.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base.
test       t/002-ttyname_r-pty.c

assertion  XSH.ttyname.003
anchor     functions/ttyname.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ttyname.html#ERRORS/ERANGE
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      2001
says       ttyname_r() with namesize equal to the string length plus one
           returns 0, terminates the string within namesize bytes, and
           does not touch the byte after the array.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base.
test       t/003-ttyname_r-exact.c

assertion  XSH.ttyname.004
anchor     functions/ttyname.html#ERRORS/ERANGE
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      2001
says       ttyname_r() with namesize equal to the string length, 1, and 0
           returns ERANGE (as the return value).
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. Contents
           of the array after the failure are not examined.
test       t/004-ttyname_r-erange.c

assertion  XSH.ttyname.005
anchor     functions/ttyname.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      2001
says       The string stored by ttyname_r() for a pseudo-terminal
           subsidiary is no longer (excluding the null byte) than
           {TTY_NAME_MAX} as reported by sysconf(_SC_TTY_NAME_MAX);
           NOTINUSE when the system reports no limit.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. Whether
           the limit counts the null byte is not settled by the text (see
           the note on Q-81), so the weaker reading is tested.
test       t/005-name-max.c

assertion  XSH.ttyname.006
anchor     functions/ttyname.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/ttyname.html#ERRORS/ENOTTY
class      required
since      1988
says       On descriptors of a regular file, both ends of a pipe and
           /dev/null, ttyname() returns a null pointer with errno set
           (non-zero), and ttyname_r() returns a non-zero error number.
note       See Q-80: the error list says "may fail", but no pathname of a
           terminal exists for such a file; the null return is the only
           reading of the RETURN VALUE text that can hold. The exact error
           number is not checked.
test       t/006-not-terminal.c

assertion  XSH.ttyname.007
anchor     functions/ttyname.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/ttyname.html#ERRORS/EBADF
class      required
since      1988
says       On -1 and on a closed descriptor, ttyname() returns a null
           pointer with errno set (non-zero), and ttyname_r() returns a
           non-zero error number.
note       See Q-80 and the preceding assertion.
test       t/007-bad-fd.c
